NAME
    cc1 - C parser (token stream -> AST)

SYNOPSIS
    cc1 src.tok >src.ast
    lex src.c | ...            (feed a token stream)

DESCRIPTION
    Reads a LEX token stream and writes the serialized abstract syntax tree
    (AST) that the code generator consumes. The AST is a whitespace-separated
    pre-order atom stream: each node is a tag followed by its fields; variadic
    lists end with a ";" terminator; a type is "<base> <ptr> <count>"; a byte
    string is "<len>" then decimal bytes. This is exactly the format the host
    reference `p8cc.py --ast` emits, reproduced byte-for-byte.

    cc1 is the last on-target pass: it completes the self-hosted C FRONT END
    (cpp | lex | cc1) that preprocesses, tokenizes and parses C natively. Code
    generation stays on the host (p8cc): the on-target back end does not fit the
    ~31.5 KB TPA. The passes chain through temp files:
        cpp foo.c  >foo.i        splice //#use libraries
        lex foo.i  >foo.tok      tokenize
        cc1 foo.tok >foo.ast     parse  (host p8cc turns the AST into asm)

    Parsing is recursive descent. The AST is emitted as it is parsed, with no
    in-memory tree of the whole program; only the current statement is buffered
    (to reorder infix operators into pre-order), so cc1 handles functions far
    larger than the TPA. All type, symbol and struct analysis is deferred to
    the code generator, keeping this pass purely syntactic.

EXAMPLES
    cc1 pwd.tok >pwd.ast     parse a tokenized source
    lex cat.i >cat.tok       then: cc1 cat.tok >cat.ast

